\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Comparison with baselines on the test set (28 patients, 38 follow-up$\rightarrow $baseline pairs). Values are mean$\pm $SD over patients (a patient's pairs are averaged first). Rank: challenge significance ranking, with the 95\,\% interval of the rank over 1000 patient bootstrap samples in brackets, over all methods except Initial, which is a reference (its MTV/TLG change is 0 by construction because nothing is resampled). Training / IO: PET and rigidity losses (PET + rig.) used in training and in instance optimisation (IO); IO ``--'': no IO, ``basic'': IO with the similarity and regularisation terms only. $^{\dagger }$/$^{\ddagger }$: Ours + IO with PET + rig. in training and IO (our full method) is significantly better/worse than this method (two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test on patient values, Holm-corrected within each column, $p<0.05$; NDV tested after the 0.005\,\% equivalence threshold). $>$ thr.: pairs with \%NDV above 0.005\,\%. Runtime per pair on one NVIDIA RTX A6000, including the affine step. Affine + IO: our instance optimisation directly after the affine pre-registration, without the network. Bold: best mean among ranked methods.}}{21}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:baselines}{{4}{21}{Comparison with baselines on the test set (28 patients, 38 follow-up$\rightarrow $baseline pairs). Values are mean$\pm $SD over patients (a patient's pairs are averaged first). Rank: challenge significance ranking, with the 95\,\% interval of the rank over 1000 patient bootstrap samples in brackets, over all methods except Initial, which is a reference (its MTV/TLG change is 0 by construction because nothing is resampled). Training / IO: PET and rigidity losses (PET + rig.) used in training and in instance optimisation (IO); IO ``--'': no IO, ``basic'': IO with the similarity and regularisation terms only. $^{\dagger }$/$^{\ddagger }$: Ours + IO with PET + rig. in training and IO (our full method) is significantly better/worse than this method (two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test on patient values, Holm-corrected within each column, $p<0.05$; NDV tested after the 0.005\,\% equivalence threshold). $>$ thr.: pairs with \%NDV above 0.005\,\%. Runtime per pair on one NVIDIA RTX A6000, including the affine step. Affine + IO: our instance optimisation directly after the affine pre-registration, without the network. Bold: best mean among ranked methods}{table.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Patient-level results on the test set (28 patients; a patient's pairs are averaged). Violins: kernel density over patients, line: median; each dot is one patient. In brackets: where the PET and rigidity losses (PET + rig.) act; basic: IO with the similarity and regularisation terms only; both: training and IO. Initial alignment is omitted (CT Dice 9.5\,\%, HD95 44.4\,mm; MTV/TLG change 0 by construction, Table\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {tab:baselines}).}}{22}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:baselines_distribution}{{4}{22}{Patient-level results on the test set (28 patients; a patient's pairs are averaged). Violins: kernel density over patients, line: median; each dot is one patient. In brackets: where the PET and rigidity losses (PET + rig.) act; basic: IO with the similarity and regularisation terms only; both: training and IO. Initial alignment is omitted (CT Dice 9.5\,\%, HD95 44.4\,mm; MTV/TLG change 0 by construction, Table~\ref {tab:baselines})}{figure.4}{}}
